  "explanation": {
    "easy": {
      "heading": "Warming Up With A Morning Coffee",
      "body": "I started today by looking for the easiest wins on the grid. The two empty cells at 1,2 and 4,0 were like a free head start, so I marked those down right away. Then I saw that cell 0,3 had to be less than 1. Since we do not have negative numbers in Pips, that one had to be a 0! It felt great to get three cells filled in just a few seconds.\n\nNext, I looked at the equals regions. The pairs at 1,1 and 2,1, and 1,3 and 2,3, really limited my choices. I saw a sum of 8 for cells 3,1 and 4,1, and looking at my domino list, the 5,3 piece seemed like the perfect fit there. Once I put that down, the rest of the dominoes like the 1,2 and 6,2 just started to slide into place. It was a really smooth solve to kick off the day.",
      "medium": {
        "heading": "Finding My Flow In The Middle",
        "body": "The medium puzzle today had some much bigger regions that looked a bit scary at first. The four cells grouped together at 0,1, 0,2, 1,1, and 1,2 all had to be equal. I looked at my dominoes and noticed I had a lot of 6s, so I kept that in mind. The empty cell at 2,0 gave me a nice anchor point to work around while I figured out where the 6,6 and 4,6 pieces might go.\n\nI spent a little extra time on the bottom right corner. There is a cluster of three cells at 3,3, 4,2, and 4,3 that all must be the same number. After checking the greater than 1 constraint at 2,3, I realized I could use the 6,1 and 3,6 dominoes to make everything balance out. It took a bit of moving things around, but once I realized how the 4,4 domino fit into the sum of 2 at 3,0, everything clicked.",
        "hard": {
          "heading": "The Ultimate Saturday Brain Teaser",
          "body": "Wow, the hard puzzle really made me work for it today! The breakthrough moment for me was looking at that long column on the right side. Cells 1,6, 2,6, and 3,6 had a sum target of 0. That is basically a gift because it means every single one of those cells must be a 0! That gave me a huge vertical line to build from. Then I tackled the sum of 10 at 1,0 and 1,1, which meant I needed two very high numbers.\n\nThe trickiest part was definitely the triple equals region at 3,4, 4,4, and 5,4. I had to carefully look at my remaining dominoes like 1,4 and 2,4 to see which ones shared a common number. I almost put the 6,6 in the wrong spot, but the greater than 3 clue at 5,6 saved me. It was like a giant jigsaw puzzle where the numbers finally behaved after my second cup of coffee.",
          "tips": "Try to find the smallest sum or less than targets first because they have the fewest possibilities. If you see a sum of 0 or a less than 1, you know those cells are all zeros. Also, always keep an eye on your domino list to see which numbers you have used up so you do not try to place a piece you already used."
        }
      },
      "tips": "Try to find the smallest sum or less than targets first because they have the fewest possibilities. If you see a sum of 0 or a less than 1, you know those cells are all zeros. Also, always keep an eye on your domino list to see which numbers you have used up so you do not try to place a piece you already used.",
      "learned": "Today I really noticed how much easier the puzzle becomes when you find a zero. Those sum of zero regions are like gold because they lock in so many cells at once. I also learned that when you have a big equals region spanning three or four cells, you should look for the pips that appear most frequently in your domino list to fill them.",
      "faqs": [
        {
          "question": "How do you play the NYT Pips puzzle?",
          "answer": "You place a set of standard dominoes onto a grid so that every cell follows the rules. Rules include sums, equal values, or being greater or less than a specific number."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can you use the same domino twice in Pips?",
          "answer": "No, you have a specific list of dominoes for each puzzle, and each one can only be used exactly once to fill the grid."
        },
        {
          "question": "What does an empty region mean in Pips?",
          "answer": "An empty region or a cell marked empty means there is no specific pip count required, but it still must be filled by part of a domino according to the other rules."
        },
        {
          "question": "What are some good strategies for hard Pips puzzles?",
          "answer": "Start with regions that have very high or very low targets. A sum of 0 or a very high sum like 10 or 12 narrows down your domino choices significantly."
        },
        {
          "question": "Are Pips puzzles the same every day?",
          "answer": "The rules stay the same, but the grid size, the domino set, and the target numbers change every day to keep things fresh and challenging."
